      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but i keep seeing people call it \"spooky action at a distance.\" why do they use that word? is there something actually weird or \"spooky\" about the math/physics of it, or is it just a metaphor?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The term \"spooky action at a distance\" was coined by Albert Einstein, who was initially skeptical of entanglement. He thought it was a bit too weird, too \"magical,\" and didn't quite understand how it worked. But the phrase has stuck, and it's now widely used to describe the phenomenon.\n\nThe math behind entanglement is indeed quite fascinating, and it's what makes it seem \"spooky.\" When two particles are entangled, their properties are correlated in a way that can't be explained by classical physics. It's like they're connected by a non-local, instantaneous link that allows them to communicate with each other, even if they're separated by vast distances.\n\nOne of the key features of entanglement is that it's a non-local phenomenon, meaning that the properties of the particles are not determined by their local environment. It's like the particles are \"talking\" to each other in a way that transcends space and time. This is what Einstein found so unsettling – it seemed to imply that information was being transmitted between the particles instantaneously, regardless of the distance between them.\n\nBut, as it turns out, the math behind entanglement is actually quite elegant and well-understood. It",
      "source": "target",
